# Grapevine — environment template.
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the real values. Never commit secrets.
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Loaded by Next.js: .env.local overrides .env. Both are gitignored; this
# .env.example is the committed source of truth for what the app expects.

# ── App ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public base URL of THIS app. Must match where the app is served.
# Local dev: http://localhost:3000   Prod: https://throughgrapevine.com
#
# The invitation email links to it as the "open it in your browser" destination, so a
# staging deploy left pointing at localhost invites parishioners to their own machine.
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Optional: serve the app under a subpath (e.g. /app). Leave blank for root.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH=

# ── API ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the client sends API requests. Baked into the bundle at build time, so it
# must be NEXT_PUBLIC_ and you must rebuild after changing it.
#
# The web build leaves this blank and falls back to same-origin /api. The NATIVE
# build (static export) has no server of its own, so it MUST point at a running
# instance by absolute URL — set this before `npm run build:native` / `sync`:
#   iOS Simulator:    http://localhost:3000/api
#   Android Emulator: http://10.0.2.2:3000/api   (localhost is the emulator itself)
#   Physical device:  http://<your-Mac-LAN-IP>:3000/api   (same Wi-Fi)
#   Deployed:         https://throughgrapevine.com/api
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=

# Extra cross-origin API callers, comma-separated. The web app is same-origin and
# needs nothing here; the native WebView is cross-origin but its capacitor:// and
# https://localhost origins are already allow-listed in src/lib/server/handler.js.
# Add a LAN IP or tunnel host here only when a device hits the API from one:
#   CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://192.168.1.20:3000,https://myapp.ngrok.app
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ── Supabase (auth) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# From your project's dashboard → Settings → API Keys.
#
# Supabase renamed its keys: `anon` → publishable (sb_publishable_...) and
# `service_role` → secret (sb_secret_...). The legacy pair still works but is
# deprecated at the end of 2026 — use the new names on a new project. The app reads
# either, preferring the new one.
#
# The URL and publishable key are NEXT_PUBLIC_ because the browser client needs
# them, and that is safe by design: the publishable key grants nothing on its own,
# and prisma/policies.sql revokes the Data API's access to every table. They are
# baked in at build time, so rebuild after changing either (and re-run
# `npm run sync` for native).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# SERVER ONLY. Bypasses every RLS policy and can create, delete, and impersonate any
# user. Never prefix it NEXT_PUBLIC_ (that is what ships a value to the browser),
# never import it outside src/lib/server/**, and never commit it. Needed to
# provision users (the seed script and the invite flow), to create the storage
# bucket, and to mint the signed upload URLs the composer uploads photos with.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...

# ── Supabase (storage) — REQUIRED before anyone can upload a photo ────────────
# Photos (post media, profile pictures) go into a Storage bucket named "media". A
# new Supabase project has no buckets, so create it once per environment:
#
#   npm run storage:setup
#
# It is idempotent and needs only the values above. It creates the bucket public-read
# with a 25MB limit and an allow-list of image and attachment types; re-running
# reconciles those settings without touching anything already uploaded.
#
# Re-run it whenever that allow-list or the size limit changes in the script — the
# bucket enforces its own copy, so a type the app accepts but the bucket does not
# fails at the upload with a mime-type error and no other clue.
#
# Public *read* is deliberate — see the header of src/lib/server/storage.js. Writes
# are not public: they require a signed upload URL, which only POST /api/uploads can
# mint, and only after checking that the caller may make the write the file is for.
#
# Nothing else needs configuring. The bucket name is not an env var because it is not
# an environment choice — it lives in src/lib/server/storage.js beside the code that
# builds paths into it.

# ── Email delivery — REQUIRED for sign-in to work ─────────────────────────────
# Sign-in is an emailed one-time code. In Supabase, email OTP and magic link are one
# implementation, and the template alone decides which is sent: the code is only
# delivered if the Magic Link template contains {{ .Token }}. The stock template has
# only {{ .ConfirmationURL }}, so until you change it no code is ever sent and the
# second step of the login screen cannot be completed.
#
# This project is on Pro, so template customisation is unrestricted. The template we
# actually send is checked in — paste `supabase/templates/magic-link.html` into
# Authentication → Email Templates → Magic Link, and set the subject beside it to:
#
#   {{ .Token }} is your Grapevine code
#
# It is in the repo because the dashboard is the only place it runs, and a template
# living in one un-versioned textarea is one careless edit away from breaking every
# sign-in. It carries no {{ .ConfirmationURL }} at all: a link dead-ends in the native
# WebView, and offering both trains people to click the one that fails.
#
# The code length is a second dashboard setting — Authentication → Sign In / Providers →
# Email → Email OTP Length. It is 6 here (a new project defaults to 8) and is mirrored as
# OTP_LENGTH in src/lib/constants.js. Change one, change the other in the same sitting.
#
# ── SMTP: Resend on throughgrapevine.com ──
# Resend, Postmark and SES all require a verified sending domain — Resend explicitly
# will not send from a shared or public one, so there is no "just testing" sender.
# throughgrapevine.com is that domain, and Resend is the provider.
#
#   Host  smtp.resend.com      Port  465 (implicit TLS; 587 STARTTLS also works)
#   User  resend               Pass  the Resend API key (re_...)
#   From  no-reply@throughgrapevine.com    Sender name  Grapevine
#
# Verify the ROOT domain in Resend, not a send.* subdomain. The usual reason to verify
# a subdomain is that a root SPF record collides with one already there — but Resend
# publishes its SPF and return-path MX at `send.throughgrapevine.com` regardless of
# which name you register, so the root's own MX and SPF are never touched and a later
# Google Workspace on @throughgrapevine.com cannot conflict. Registering the root is
# what lets the code arrive from no-reply@throughgrapevine.com rather than from
# no-reply@send.throughgrapevine.com, which is the address a parishioner reads.
#
# Three DNS records, all values copied from Resend's domain page (the DKIM key is
# generated per domain, so it cannot be written down here):
#
#   MX    send                  feedback-smtp.<region>.amazonses.com   priority 10
#   TXT   send                  v=spf1 include:amazonses.com ~all
#   TXT   resend._domainkey     p=<key from Resend>
#
# Add DMARC too — `_dmarc` TXT `v=DMARC1; p=none; rua=mailto:you@throughgrapevine.com`.
# Gmail and Yahoo demand it of bulk senders and OTP volume here is nowhere near bulk,
# but a domain with no DMARC record at all is scored worse, and p=none only asks for
# reports. If the host is Cloudflare, the DKIM TXT must be DNS-only, not proxied.
#
# Sending starts at 30 messages/hour under Authentication → Rate Limits, which is the
# number to raise before onboarding a parish — a roster being added in one sitting is a
# burst of first sign-ins, and the 31st gets 429 over_email_send_rate_limit.
#
# For day-to-day development prefer `npm run dev:login` anyway: it mints a sign-in code
# through the Admin API and prints it, which works on the web and on a device and
# consumes no email quota. See native-architecture.md → Testing sign-in on a device.
#
# SMTP credentials live in the Supabase dashboard — Authentication → Emails → SMTP
# Settings — not here. Everything above this line is documentation; the two variables
# below are configuration.
#
# ── The invitation email — OPTIONAL ──
# Everything above is Supabase mailing sign-in codes. This is the app's own letter: when
# an admin adds somebody who has never signed in, they are sent the download links and
# told to sign in with the address it arrived at. It has to exist because the app is
# invite-only — `shouldCreateUser: false` means a person who has not been provisioned
# cannot even ask for a code, so somebody admitted a minute ago has a working account
# and no way to learn it exists.
#
# It goes out over the Resend HTTP API rather than SMTP, from the same Resend account and
# the same verified domain as the codes above. The key is the `re_...` value you already
# used as the SMTP password.
#
# Leave it unset and `emailConfigured()` in src/lib/server/email.js is false: admission
# still works, the membership is still written, the notification row is still there, and
# only the letter is missing. Somebody who has signed in before is told by push instead,
# which needs nothing here.
#
# Sending is a queue, not part of the request: admission writes the `Invite` row owing a
# letter and `sendOwedInvites` delivers it, swept off GET /api/notifications/unread. A
# refusal (a blown daily quota, a bad key) leaves the row owed and retries it on a
# backoff, so setting this key later sends the letters that were queued without it.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# The From header. Must be an address on a domain verified in Resend, or every send is
# rejected. Defaults to the value below when unset.
# EMAIL_FROM="Grapevine <no-reply@throughgrapevine.com>"

# ── Seed sign-in accounts ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two addresses `npm run prisma:seed` provisions as real, sign-innable users:
# an ADMIN priest who can publish, and a MEMBER layperson who cannot.
#
# Set these to addresses you can actually receive mail at — signing in means reading
# a code out of an inbox, so a fictional address is an account you can never use.
# Plus-addressing makes this easy: you@gmail.com and you+member@gmail.com are two
# distinct accounts landing in one inbox.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=
SEED_MEMBER_EMAIL=

# ── Database (Prisma) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ── FOR DEVELOPMENT, POINT THESE AT THE CONTAINER ──
# `npm run db:up` starts a local Postgres (docker-compose.yml). Use it:
#
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://grapevine:grapevine@127.0.0.1:54322/grapevine
#   DIRECT_URL=postgresql://grapevine:grapevine@127.0.0.1:54322/grapevine
#
# Both the same URL, because a local Postgres is not behind a pooler — the two only
# differ in production, and only because Supabase puts one in front.
#
# Then: `npm run prisma:migrate && npm run db:secure && npm run db:seed`.
#
# The container isolates Postgres and nothing else. Auth and Storage stay pointed at
# the hosted project, so the same accounts sign in and `npm run dev:login` still works
# — see the header of docker-compose.yml for what that does and does not buy you.
#
# `npm run db:seed` and `npm run db:reset` REFUSE to run against a non-local host.
# They rewrite fixture data and drop tables respectively, and the only thing naming
# their target is the variable below. `npm run db:seed:prod` is the one meant for
# production; it writes two admins and one group and contains no delete at all.
#
# ── The production values ──
# Both from Supabase → Settings → Database → Connection string.
#
# Two URLs, and the split is required rather than an optimization:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  The pooler the app queries through at runtime. WHICH pooler is not a
#                 preference, it is decided by how the target runs — see below.
#   DIRECT_URL    A direct/session connection (port 5432), ALWAYS, whatever the target.
#                 Migrations only. Prisma Migrate takes advisory locks and issues DDL,
#                 neither of which survives a transaction pooler — point migrations at
#                 6543 and they hang or fail with prepared-statement errors.
#
# ── Choosing DATABASE_URL's port ──
# Supabase allots this database user 15 clients in SESSION mode, and session mode holds
# one of them per Prisma connection for that connection's whole life.
#
#   Long-lived Node server (`next start`, `next dev`) — SESSION pooler, port 5432.
#       One process, one bounded pool, comfortably inside the 15. Earns prepared
#       statements and one round trip per query. Set connection_limit yourself: Prisma's
#       default is num_physical_cpus * 2 + 1, which is 33 on a 16-core machine and twice
#       the allotment. 5 leaves room for `prisma studio` (it reads this same URL and
#       opens a second pool of the same size) and for db:seed / erase / owner.
#
#   Serverless (Vercel, Lambda) — TRANSACTION pooler, port 6543, ?pgbouncer=true
#   &connection_limit=1.
#       Each warm instance holds its own pool and releases nothing between invocations,
#       so session mode is exhausted at a concurrency of one or two: six instances at a
#       limit of five want thirty clients out of fifteen. Every route calls
#       requireViewer, so the whole API 500s at once with PrismaClientInitializationError
#       / FATAL: (EMAXCONNSESSION). Raising connection_limit multiplies by the instance
#       count and buys exhaustion sooner; it is not the lever it looks like. The price is
#       real, and is why session mode is preferred wherever it fits: transaction mode
#       disables prepared statements and wraps every query in BEGIN/DEALLOCATE ALL/
#       COMMIT, ~2.5x the round trips for identical SQL.
#
# Setting these in a dashboard rather than a file: paste the value WITHOUT the quotes
# shown below, and if you set it from a shell, quote the whole string or `&` truncates it.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?connection_limit=5&pool_timeout=20"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?connection_limit=1"

# ── Firebase Cloud Messaging (push notifications) — OPTIONAL ─────────────────
# SERVER ONLY, and the API runs perfectly well without any of it. When these are
# unset, `pushConfigured()` in src/lib/server/push.js is false and every send is a
# no-op: notification rows are still written, the panel behind the bell still shows
# them, and only the buzz is missing. Push must never be a reason a like fails.
#
# All three come from ONE file: Firebase console → Project settings → Service
# accounts → "Generate new private key". It downloads a JSON blob; these are the
# `project_id`, `client_email`, and `private_key` fields of it.
#
# Do not commit the JSON, and never prefix any of these NEXT_PUBLIC_ — the private
# key can mint pushes to every device in every parish.
FCM_PROJECT_ID=your-firebase-project-id
FCM_CLIENT_EMAIL=firebase-adminsdk-xxxxx@your-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com
# The `private_key` field verbatim, INCLUDING its literal \n escapes and the BEGIN/END
# lines, wrapped in double quotes. src/lib/server/push.js unescapes them before handing
# the key to `importPKCS8`, which rejects it outright otherwise — the usual symptom of
# getting this wrong is a startup that looks fine and every send failing with an
# unhelpful "Invalid keyData" the first time somebody likes a post.
FCM_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIEv...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"

# ── Firebase Remote Config (feature flags) — OPTIONAL, same credential ───────
# The three variables above are also what src/lib/server/config.js reads the app's
# feature flags with, and nothing extra goes in this file for them. The generated
# `firebase-adminsdk-*` account already carries the Firebase Admin SDK Administrator
# Service Agent role, which covers reading AND publishing a Remote Config template
# (verified against the live API). A hand-made service account would need "Firebase
# Remote Config Admin" granted under Google Cloud → IAM.
#
# Create two parameters under Firebase console → Remote Config. Both are global:
# the REST template this server reads carries each parameter's DEFAULT value only, because
# Remote Config evaluates its conditions on the client SDK, which this app does not use.
#
#   messaging_enabled   BOOLEAN   true
#       Direct messages. False takes the nav entry, the send icon and the contact list
#       away, and `requireMessaging` answers 503 FEATURE_DISABLED to every /api/messages
#       route, so it is a real control rather than hidden chrome. Nobody is ever TOLD it is
#       off: that code is silent on the client, so the app simply looks like one that never
#       had messaging.
#
#   minimum_version     STRING    (unset)
#       The oldest app version still allowed to run, as three dotted numbers ("0.1.15").
#       A NATIVE build below it opens on a drawer it cannot dismiss. Anything else — unset,
#       a blank, a typo — is read as no floor at all, deliberately: a mistyped value here
#       would otherwise wall every phone in every parish out of the app at once.
#       `npm run release:android -- --force-user-update` is the other way to set it.
#
# GET /api/config serves both, to anybody, with no session. That is the one unauthenticated
# route in the app, and it is one because a version floor readable only by a client healthy
# enough to sign in cannot be read in the case it exists for. Nothing viewer-scoped may ride
# on it. Leave all of this unconfigured and the flags resolve to their defaults: messaging on,
# no floor. Every failure here fails open, on purpose.
#
# HOW LONG A FLIP TAKES: about a minute. The server holds an answer for 60s (TTL_MS in
# src/lib/server/config.js) and the client re-reads on launch, on foregrounding, and on
# regaining a connection. The lease is PER PROCESS, so restarting `next dev` appears to make a
# change instant because the restart discarded it — and on Vercel each warm instance holds its
# own, so for up to a minute different requests can answer differently. That is bounded by the
# TTL and is not a development quirk.

# The DEVICE half of push is not configured here at all. It is two files Firebase
# generates per app, committed into the native projects rather than read from the
# environment, because the native SDKs load them at launch and not from JS:
#
#   android/app/google-services.json     (Firebase console → Android app)
#   ios/App/App/GoogleService-Info.plist (Firebase console → iOS app, then added to
#                                         the Xcode target — dragging it into the
#                                         folder is NOT enough)
#
# Neither is a secret; both are per-Firebase-project, so a staging project and a
# production one need different builds. iOS additionally needs an APNs auth key
# (.p8) uploaded to Firebase → Project settings → Cloud Messaging, the Push
# Notifications capability in Xcode, and a physical device — APNs does not work on
# the simulator. See .claude/skills/develop/native-architecture.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Daily Read
#
# Both are optional and both have working defaults — the feature needs no
# configuration at all. They exist so a deployment can pin the reading to a fork
# or a mirror if either upstream moves or goes down.
#
# The reading is the Prologue of Ohrid, fetched per request from the open-source
# ochrid.org content tree (one MDX file per day) and parsed by
# src/lib/server/prologue/parse.js. It is fetched rather than vendored: that
# corpus is CC BY-NC 4.0 and carries a third-party translation under a fair-use
# claim, so no copy of it lives in this repo. Point this at a fork's raw base if
# you need to pin a revision — the path shape `<base>/<month>/<ordinal>.mdx`
# must hold.
PROLOGUE_SOURCE_BASE_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jaredef/ochrid/main/content

# The day's fasting rule and liturgical title, from orthocal.info. The app reads
# the `gregorian` (new calendar) endpoint under this base. Unreachable is a
# supported state: the strip is simply omitted and the reading still renders.
ORTHOCAL_BASE_URL=https://orthocal.info/api

# ── Google Play publishing — OPTIONAL, LOCAL ONLY ────────────────────────────
# Read by `npm run release:android` (scripts/release-android.mjs) and by nothing
# the app ever runs. Leave unset and the script still builds and signs a bundle
# under --dry; only the upload needs credentials.
#
# Play Console → Users and permissions → invite a service account, grant it
# "Release to testing tracks", then in Google Cloud → IAM → Service Accounts →
# Keys → Add key (JSON). Point this at the downloaded file; a path outside the
# repo is the right place for it.
#
# This key can publish to the store as us. It belongs on the release machine and
# nowhere else — never in CI without a hardware-backed secret store, and never
# prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_.
PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY=/Users/you/keys/grapevine-play.json

# The same credential split into two variables, for a host that hands out strings
# rather than files. PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY wins if both are set. The private key
# keeps its literal \n escapes and its BEGIN/END lines, exactly as FCM_PRIVATE_KEY
# above does, and for the same reason — `importPKCS8` rejects it otherwise.
# PLAY_CLIENT_EMAIL=grapevine-release@your-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com
# PLAY_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIEv...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"

# The upload keystore is NOT configured here. Gradle reads it from
# android/keystore.properties (untracked) or from GRAPEVINE_STORE_FILE /
# _STORE_PASSWORD / _KEY_ALIAS / _KEY_PASSWORD, because it is consumed by the
# Android build rather than by Node. See android/app/build.gradle.

# ── Store review sign-in — OPTIONAL, TEMPORARY ───────────────────────────────
# Lets an app-store reviewer sign in with a fixed code instead of an emailed one.
# Unset either variable and POST /api/auth/review 404s, which is the normal state
# for a deploy — set them for a submission and unset them once review is done.
#
# The app is invite-only (`shouldCreateUser: false`), so a reviewer cannot sign
# up and cannot be sent a code by asking. `npm run reviewer -- <email> --org <slug>`
# creates the account; these let the person at it get in without the inbox.
#
# What the route can do is deliberately narrow: it NEVER mints a session. It
# returns Supabase's real current OTP for this ONE address, and the ordinary
# verifyOtp flow does the sign-in — so there is no second way to obtain a session
# anywhere in the app, and no address but this one can be selected.
#
# Whoever holds REVIEW_OTP can sign in as this account. Point REVIEW_EMAIL at an
# ordinary MEMBER of a demo group, never at an ADMIN or a platform OWNER.
# REVIEW_EMAIL=review@throughgrapevine.com
# MUST be exactly 6 digits (OTP_LENGTH). The login screen's code field is an
# InputOTP with exactly that many slots, so a longer code cannot be typed at all
# and the route is never reached, which looks like a broken app to the one person
# who must not see that.
#
# Six digits is a million combinations, so treat this as temporary: it guards an
# unprivileged demo account, and BOTH variables should be unset once review ends.
# Generate, don't invent:
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomInt(100000, 1000000))"
# REVIEW_OTP=

# ── Sign-up reminders — OPTIONAL ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Turns on POST /api/signups/reminders, which sends every sign-up nudge that has
# come due. Unset, that endpoint 404s — which is the normal state for a deploy, and
# does NOT mean reminders stop working.
#
# They go out either way. `sweepDueReminders` runs off the back of the bell's unread
# tally, throttled to once every few minutes, because a feature that quietly does
# nothing until somebody finds an environment variable is broken for everybody who
# did not. What this secret buys is *punctuality*: a schedule sends a nudge within a
# minute of it falling due, where the lazy sweep sends it the next time anybody in
# the parish has the app open. For a reminder a week or a day ahead that difference
# rarely matters, which is why it is optional.
#
# Why an endpoint and not a script, when everything else here is a script: sending
# a reminder writes a Notification row AND pushes to FCM, and both live under
# src/lib/server/**, every file of which starts with `import "server-only"` — a
# specifier a plain Node process cannot resolve. A script would need a second copy
# of the notification write and of the FCM transport. `npm run signups:remind` is
# a dozen lines that POST here instead.
#
# Point a cron at it every fifteen minutes. It claims the rows it is about to send
# before it sends them, so two overlapping runs cannot nudge anybody twice, and it
# retires anything more than six hours overdue rather than delivering it late.
#
#     */15 * * * * curl -fsS -X POST \
#         -H "Authorization: Bearer $REMINDERS_SECRET" \
#         https://throughgrapevine.com/api/signups/reminders
#
# Generate, don't invent:  openssl rand -hex 32
# REMINDERS_SECRET=
